WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.020
Good to have you on Planetarian Daily, episode 93 for June 18, 2026.

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:11.080
Let's get into today's research and health developments. A spatial atlas now maps protein

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:18.920
locations across 13,000 proteins in 3,000 human samples spanning 58 tissues and 25 cancers.

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:26.180
Researchers generated high-resolution spatial maps from roughly 3,000 samples across 58 tissues

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:32.660
and 25 cancers. The study quantified protein abundance and localization at high resolution

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:38.300
across both healthy and diseased states. The resulting data set provides a reference for how

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:44.060
protein organization differs between normal tissue architecture and tumor micro-environments.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:49.540
This resource can now be used to identify spatially restricted biomarkers or therapeutic

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:56.220
targets that bulk proteomics approaches miss. Follow-up work will likely integrate these maps

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:02.580
with single-cell transcriptomic and metabolomic layers. From tissue scale protein organization,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:09.140
we turn to another genome level insight into metabolic specialization. Sequencing of multiple

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:15.280
slot species identified expanded gene families that encode mitochondrial proteins involved in

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.980
energy metabolism. The duplications may help explain the animals' characteristically low

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:27.060
metabolic rate and slow movement. Researchers are now examining whether similar expansions occurred

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:33.060
in other low energy mammals. The finding links genome architecture directly to physiological

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:39.620
specialization. Another crop genome now offers similar precision for breeding decisions.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:46.200
The new assembly resolves all chromosomes and captures both cultivated and wild peanut subgenomes

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.400
in a single reference. High-quality annotation now allows precise mapping of genes controlling

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:59.120
oil composition, allergenicity, and drought response. Breeders can use the sequence to accelerate

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.700
marker-assisted selection for improved varieties. The work also clarifies the polyploidization

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.860
event that gave rise to modern peanut. While genomes reveal what is possible, new tools are now

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:17.800
automating how we measure individual particles. Researchers at the University of Gothenburg

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:23.380
and Chalmers University trained an AI system to operate optical tweezers that capture,

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:30.160
measure, and sort hundreds of microscopic particles per hour. The platform performs continuous

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:36.820
sample loading, trapping, and data collection in a closed loop. By removing manual intervention,

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:43.200
the method increases throughput for single particle biophysical assays. The approach is now being

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:50.020
applied to study protein complexes and extracellular vesicles. From lab automation, we move to

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:56.700
organisms that endure extreme natural conditions without any assistance. South African lead sampling

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:03.040
showed that sea ice microbial communities remain metabolically active through polar winter by

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:09.480
synthesizing and catabolizing, compatible solutes that stabilize proteins and membranes under

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.480
extreme cold and salinity. These compounds allow the microbes to persist in a dormant,

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:22.560
yet viable state until spring light returns. The findings refine models of how polar microbial

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:28.520
loops contribute to carbon and nutrient cycling. Cold tolerance in microbes contrasts with

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:35.800
heat vulnerability in certain bees. A continent-wide survey of Australian native bees found that species

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:42.200
nesting in plant stems lacked the thermal buffering provided by underground nests. Tropical stem

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:48.720
nesters were particularly at risk even though they already inhabit hot climates. The study measured

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:54.780
nest temperatures and bee activity thresholds across multiple biomes. Behavioral traits,

00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:00.560
such as nest depth and material choice, emerged as stronger predictors of survival than geographic

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:07.140
origin alone. Eight common food additives have been associated with higher cardiovascular risk

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:14.020
according to recent analysis. More than 112,000 participants were followed for up to eight years

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:20.800
in the study. Higher intake of specific preservatives correlated with elevated incidents of hypertension,

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:27.720
myocardial infarction and stroke. Adjustments for diet quality and lifestyle factors did not

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:34.460
remove the associations. These preservatives are widely used in processed foods to extend shelf life.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:40.520
The authors call for updated exposure limits based on these long-term observational data.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:46.820
From wild bees, we shift to everyday urban environments and the life they support below ground.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.500
Most people assume city environments are relatively sterile compared with forests or farms.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:59.200
In reality, the amount of visible greenery along streets strongly shapes the diversity of bacteria

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:05.060
living in nearby soils and on surfaces. Right now, as you walk through an urban neighborhood,

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:11.500
the density of trees and shrubs you see is quietly influencing which microbes can persist in

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:17.600
that patch of ground. A recent study in Taipei found that neighborhood scoring higher on a simple

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:23.540
green-view index hosted measurably richer bacterial communities, even after accounting for

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:29.920
parks and land use. Each incremental increase in visible greenery tracked with higher microbial

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:36.880
richness, a pattern that held across an entire metropolitan area. The practical takeaway is that

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:43.040
small-scale planting decisions such as street trees, green walls, and pocket parks can measurably

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:48.980
affect the microbial environment people encounter daily. From space-exposed plants, we turn to

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:54.980
cognitive variation already present in our closest relatives. Hundreds of wine grape seeds from

00:05:54.980 --> 00:06:01.280
Texas A&amp;M will orbit for approximately six months. They will receive continuous cosmic radiation

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:07.920
exposure before return to earth. After landing, the seeds will be planted and grown to assess

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:14.100
heritable changes in growth, fruit, chemistry, and stress tolerance. The project aims to produce

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:20.180
the first wine from space-exposed grape lineages. Researchers expect the radiation dose to

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:26.620
induce novel genetic variation not easily generated by terrestrial breeding. From ape cognition,

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:32.980
we move to the quiet, mathematical lessons children encounter at home. Comparative testing across

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:39.000
multiple ape species revealed consistent within species variation in problem-solving approaches

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:45.880
and social learning strategies. Some individuals relied more on individual exploration while others

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:52.000
copied conspecifics. The pattern mirrors human cognitive diversity and suggests these differences

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:58.200
predate the human chimpanzee split. Larger sample sizes across field sites are now being collected

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:03.860
to test heritability. From household learning, we close with a national effort to account for

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:10.220
ocean health. Observations in family kitchens documented frequent, spontaneous, mathematical reasoning

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:15.640
during cooking, measuring, and sorting tasks. Children encountered concepts of quantity,

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:21.880
proportion, and pattern without explicit instruction. The study recorded these interactions across

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:27.660
multiple households and age groups. Parents were often unaware they were providing foundational

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:34.300
mathematical experiences. A multidisciplinary team produced an ocean accounting white paper

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:39.700
that integrates ecological, economic, and social indicators for marine management.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:46.220
The framework will feed into the 2026 to 2036 national marine science strategy.

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:51.560
Pilot accounts are being tested in several coastal regions to quantify trade-offs between

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:58.020
conservation and resource use. The approach aims to make ocean health data usable for policy

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:05.160
decisions at multiple scales. The complete spatial proteomics atlas splice tissue and cell level maps

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:11.760
of more than 13,000 proteins across healthy and cancerous samples. It reveals how protein

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:17.880
localization changes in tumors compared with matched normal tissue, offering a new layer of

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:24.740
resolution beyond bulk measurements. Cancer biologists can now query whether a candidate target is

00:08:24.740 --> 00:08:31.060
confined to specific tumor regions or broadly distributed. The next step is integration with

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:38.180
ongoing single cell and spatial transcriptomic data sets to build multi-omic atlases that guide

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:45.480
precision dosing and biopsy interpretation. The spatial proteomics atlas and recent genomic studies

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:52.220
illustrate how spatial and sequence data convert previously invisible biology into measurable reference

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:57.700
maps. Next time we'll be watching for updates on how the ocean accounting framework performs in

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:04.380
its first pilot regions. That's Planetarian Daily for today. If you enjoyed this, a rating or review

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:11.000
on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps new listeners find the show. I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

